<|start_of_role|>assistant<|end_of_role|>Here's a Python implementation of the quicksort algorithm:

```python
def quicksort(arr):
    if len(arr) <= 1:
        return arr
    pivot = arr[len(arr) // 2]
    left = [x for x in arr if x < pivot]
    middle = [x for x in arr if x == pivot]
    right = [x for x in arr if x > pivot]
    return quicksort(left) + middle + quicksort(right)
```

The time complexity of quicksort is O(n log n) in the average and best cases, where n is the number of elements in the input array. This is because quicksort follows a divide-and-conquer strategy, recursively sorting the left and right subarrays around a pivot element. The average case occurs when the pivot is chosen as the median of the array, ensuring that the subarrays are roughly equal in size. In the best case, when the input array is already sorted, quicksort performs exceptionally well, achieving a time complexity of O(n log n). However, in the worst-case scenario, when the input array is sorted in reverse order or contains many duplicate elements, the time complexity degrades to O



